            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"NOVEL\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The pearl primarily symbolizes the Kingdom of Heaven, salvation, or supreme spiritual treasure, based heavily on the Parable of the Pearl of Great Price. Secondary meanings include sacred truths, Christ, the Church, or worldly luxury.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The pearl symbolizes the full moon marking an appointed month, with the twelve pearl gates of the New Jerusalem corresponding to the twelve months.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Supreme spiritual treasure, typically the Kingdom of Heaven, salvation, or the Gospel.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The full moon and its role in marking appointed months.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"NO_CONSENSUS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book completely abandons the consensus focus on spiritual valuation and redemptive metaphors, instead assigning the pearl a cosmological and calendrical referent.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"While the consensus views the pearl as a metaphor for something of incomparable spiritual worth (or alternately, vain worldly wealth), the book claims the pearl is a symbol for the full moon.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book's identification of the pearl as the full moon is entirely absent from the consensus responses. The book acknowledges the common view in its notes but replaces it with a completely novel calendrical interpretation.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"INSUFFICIENT\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"None of the cited scriptures directly identify a pearl with the moon. The connection requires an unstated, external typological leap synthesizing the twelve pearl gates of Revelation 21 with verses about lunar phases and appointed times (Psalms 81:3; 104:19).\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
